trek -- Star Trek, the command-line game from Berkeley=====================================================    trekYou command the Enterprise.  Choose a game length (short, medium, long),a skill (novice to impossible) and a password, and the galaxy is dealt:so many Klingons, so many starbases, and the stardates you have to clearthe Klingons before time runs out.Everything happens at the `Command:' prompt.  `s' is the short-rangesensor scan of your quadrant -- the Enterprise is E, starbases #, stars *,Klingons K -- with the ship's status beside it; `l' is the long-range scanof the quadrants around you.  `m' moves and `w' sets warp, `p' firesphasers and `t' torpedoes, `sh' raises or lowers shields, `do' docks at astarbase to repair and refuel, `c' is the computer.  `help' lists everycommand; `dump' saves the game to trek.dump; `terminate' ends it.The C version by Eric P. Allman, UC Berkeley, 1976, as 4.3BSD Net/2carried it, from The Unix Heritage Society's archive.  Full originals inSRC/trek/ORIG; its troff manual is SRC/trek/DOC/trekmanual.nr.